We are live, Kate, what are you doing?

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:10.269
Just seeing when the next iPhone is coming
out.

00:00:10.269 --> 00:00:11.269
The next?

00:00:11.269 --> 00:00:14.200
Well, yeah, I missed the last one, and so
I figured I'd just jump onto the 15.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:16.640
You've seen the old brick I've got here.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:19.810
Maybe that's a good idea, because the next
one might not be made in China.

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:23.470
I mean, it might not be made in India, but
it could be assembled in India.

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:24.720
Why is that?

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:29.740
That's because, you know, COVID and they were
lockdowns and the whole supply chain suffered.

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:30.740
That's true.

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:34.570
And now we saw over Christmas, the production
was limited.

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:39.579
And so, it seems like China is missing out
and other countries can't really wait so long.

00:00:39.579 --> 00:00:41.820
So maybe they're going to look to diversify.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:42.820
Yeah.

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:45.900
And that diversifying might be good for India.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:48.649
Which means jobs, possibly.

00:00:48.649 --> 00:00:49.780
That is right.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:55.949
So, the question we're going to ask today
is: I s diversifying from China creating jobs

00:00:55.949 --> 00:00:56.949
in India?

00:00:56.949 --> 00:00:57.949
All right.

00:00:57.949 --> 00:01:02.710
Welcome to Global Eyes, where we take a deep
dive into the security policy issues affecting

00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:04.860
your lives and our lives.

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:05.970
I'm Isha.

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:07.630
And I'm Kate.

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:10.950
So, today we're going to talk about chips
and wafers.

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:12.070
Excuse me?

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:16.500
Not the snacks - chips and wafers, but the
semiconductor chips.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:21.090
They are everywhere like these tiny things,
they are in our smartphones, they are in laptops,

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:22.579
washing machines, cars…

00:01:22.579 --> 00:01:23.579
Toothbrushes!

00:01:23.579 --> 00:01:26.299
Toothbrushes, imagine they're everywhere.

00:01:26.299 --> 00:01:29.310
And you just can't imagine a world without
them today.

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:35.549
So the thing is that they have become an integral
part of the functioning of any society today.

00:01:35.549 --> 00:01:40.110
And mainland China is the fastest growing
chip producer in the world.

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:43.890
And Taiwan is home to the most important chip
factory in the world.

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:48.420
And it's called Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing
Company, or TSMC for short.

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:50.960
I prefer the shortened version of TSMC.

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:51.960
Right.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:57.049
So now, if for any reason China decides to
stop the production or if the production stops

00:01:57.049 --> 00:02:02.579
like it happened in COVID, now the whole globe
is going to be affected by that.

00:02:02.579 --> 00:02:03.680
And that's the big deal.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:07.920
We've seen that during the pandemic: In Germany,
I know a lot of people who are not able to

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:08.959
buy cars now.

00:02:08.959 --> 00:02:12.650
These huge chunks of steel, they're actually
there, but you can't use them.

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:15.500
They're useless because those semiconductor
chips, they're not there.

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:17.370
They were not coming from China.

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:18.959
So that's the thing.

00:02:18.959 --> 00:02:24.230
If people don't diversify, the world would
come to a standstill.

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:25.230
Exactly.

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:28.750
And I think now we can go to our first guest
and understand things in a better manner.

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:33.790
I just wanted to give a little briefing so
that we know what we're really talking about.

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:37.120
But I guess our first guest can actually make
us understand it better.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:41.500
So let's find out exactly what that means
and especially for India.

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:43.470
So let's bring in our first guest.

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:47.690
With us now we have Mr. Arjun Gargeyas, who's
currently based in Delhi.

00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:54.290
He is an expert at high tech geopolitics and
a consultant for the Indian Ministry of Electronics

00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:56.040
and Information Technology.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:58.450
Thank you so much for joining us on Global
Eyes.

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:00.760
Hello, nice to meet you.

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:03.080
And nice to be here.

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:09.110
Now, Arjun has written this wonderful paper
on silicon diplomacy, and I can highly recommend

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:10.110
it.

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:11.500
I must say, Kate and I, we really enjoyed
reading it.

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:15.610
We learnt a lot of things and we also got
confused about a lot of things.

00:03:15.610 --> 00:03:18.920
And so let's start talking about the paper
first.

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:24.900
So Arjun, there are a lot of nitty gritties,
like you've started the paper with that: semiconductors,

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:26.140
transistors, chips.

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:30.940
Now these are terms that we use loosely; I
don't think everybody realizes while talking

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:32.800
about it, what they're actually talking about.

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:37.330
So if you could just explain that first of
all, so that we can put this in perspective

00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:39.870
that what exactly is happening in China?

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:41.220
What is happening in Taiwan?

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:43.650
And where does India fit into this whole picture?

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:45.019
Sure, sure.

00:03:45.019 --> 00:03:48.160
I mean, to give you a primer, right?

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:54.239
I think people started talking about semiconductors
and chips, especially during the COVID 19

00:03:54.239 --> 00:03:57.420
pandemic when there was a global shortage.

00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:02.769
But clearly, the definition of a chip, of
semiconductor material and all kind of got

00:04:02.769 --> 00:04:03.860
mixed up.

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:10.680
And that is where, you know, the policy perspective
for any person kind of got muddled.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:18.699
Just to put things in perspective, a semiconductor
is just another material.

00:04:18.699 --> 00:04:26.820
It is from the silicon family that can be
silicon germanium, silicon carbide, then gallium

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:27.820
nitride.

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:37.800
These are materials which are basically in
between conductors and insulators, and they

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:41.740
are used as substrate or just like wafers
in chips.

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:50.729
Chips are what we use in all our regular electronic
products: laptops, mobile phones, anything,

00:04:50.729 --> 00:05:05.270
iPod, basically anything which we use that
has a chip in it.

00:05:05.270 --> 00:05:13.990
Now having said that, what a semiconductor
is, what a chip is and what a transistor is,

00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:19.490
what is being produced in China, what is being
produced in Taiwan, and where does India come

00:05:19.490 --> 00:05:20.580
in?

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:21.669
Okay.

00:05:21.669 --> 00:05:28.669
So if you talk about the production of a chip
itself, it undergoes multiple handovers in

00:05:28.669 --> 00:05:35.670
terms of the raw material, which is the semiconductor
material has different place....

00:05:35.670 --> 00:05:41.960
Basically different places have different
deposits of raw materials, like China has

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:45.770
a high deposit of silicon and even the US.

00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:49.470
So that is basically the starting point of
it.

00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:51.440
That's the raw material, right?

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:55.160
And then you design the s and then you manufacture
the chip.

00:05:55.160 --> 00:06:03.400
So let's go by the four basic phases of how
you manufacture the chip itself or how you

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:04.400
make the product.

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:09.290
One is the raw materials, which is the semiconductor
materials.

00:06:09.290 --> 00:06:14.900
Then is the design phase wherein you have
engineers kind of using software to place

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:22.870
these kinds of transistors and on the chip
itself and kind of design it in a way that

00:06:22.870 --> 00:06:24.160
they can communicate with each other.

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:26.699
And then comes the manufacturing or the fabrication.

00:06:26.699 --> 00:06:32.020
But this is where you kind of use high precision
materials, high precision equipment.

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:39.870
Taiwan is the powerhouse in the manufacturing
phase.

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:44.740
So in the manufacturing phase, they are the
powerhouse in the world.

00:06:44.740 --> 00:06:50.259
So they manufacture over 60% of the world's
total chips.

00:06:50.259 --> 00:06:54.900
And this is because they run on what we call
as the pure play foundry model.

00:06:54.900 --> 00:07:02.030
So what that is basically is that the fact
that Taiwanese firms, they are not into any

00:07:02.030 --> 00:07:06.159
of the other aspects of the supply chain,
they just do the manufacturing bit.

00:07:06.159 --> 00:07:12.840
So they take in designs from American firms,
some South Korean firms, some Japanese firms,

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:14.370
some Chinese firms.

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:18.330
And then they take the designs and then they
manufacture it and give back the product to

00:07:18.330 --> 00:07:19.330
them.

00:07:19.330 --> 00:07:20.330
So that is all they do.

00:07:20.330 --> 00:07:23.780
Now, China is trying to do all aspects, right.

00:07:23.780 --> 00:07:28.319
So they believe in being self-sufficient across
the supply chain.

00:07:28.319 --> 00:07:34.980
But it is hard, as we know, that each phase
has different kind of requirements, different

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:37.919
kind of expertise required.

00:07:37.919 --> 00:07:44.150
So it's just the fact that China has been
trying to do it and has kind of made some

00:07:44.150 --> 00:07:45.569
kind of gains.

00:07:45.569 --> 00:07:53.530
But where they are most concentrated right
now is in the assembly, test and packaging

00:07:53.530 --> 00:07:55.490
aspect.

00:07:55.490 --> 00:08:05.530
So they do the large part of it after the
chip is manufactured.

00:08:05.530 --> 00:08:09.129
You see big Chinese factories assembling products,
putting these chips into certain electronic

00:08:09.129 --> 00:08:10.129
products.

00:08:10.129 --> 00:08:16.150
Apple has one of their biggest assembly-like
factories in China.

00:08:16.150 --> 00:08:18.940
So China has been doing that itself.

00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:25.300
And they're also trying to get into the manufacturing
bit, because they clearly know that the world

00:08:25.300 --> 00:08:33.110
is heavily dependent on Taiwan, and they have
kind of made their own manufacturing industry.

00:08:33.110 --> 00:08:44.039
SMIC is their main contract manufacturer,
wherein they kind of manufacture these chips

00:08:44.039 --> 00:08:45.630
for local domestic semiconductor firms.

00:08:45.630 --> 00:08:48.970
So, I mean, that's the thing right now.

00:08:48.970 --> 00:08:54.780
And when it comes to India right now and going
forward, what kind of role are we expect India

00:08:54.780 --> 00:08:57.230
to play?

00:08:57.230 --> 00:09:04.420
So India has been trying to build a fabrication
facility or a manufacturing facility from,

00:09:04.420 --> 00:09:07.100
let's say, almost 40 years.

00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:09.420
It's almost from the 1970s, 1980s.

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:10.420
They've been trying to do it.

00:09:10.420 --> 00:09:16.460
But so far till now, they have not been able
to build a commercial fabrication facility.

00:09:16.460 --> 00:09:23.240
There are a few fabs or what we call manufacturing
centers in India.

00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:28.029
But these are very, very specific and they
cater only to the defense industry, the space

00:09:28.029 --> 00:09:32.649
industry, which is basically the government
owned sector.

00:09:32.649 --> 00:09:36.410
There is no commercial fab that is operating
in the country and they've been trying to

00:09:36.410 --> 00:09:37.410
do it.

00:09:37.410 --> 00:09:40.870
But their current strength actually lies in
the design aspect.

00:09:40.870 --> 00:09:47.089
So you see that Indian engineers are at the
forefront of designing these chips.

00:09:47.089 --> 00:09:50.089
These are electronics engineers, myself being
one.

00:09:50.089 --> 00:09:54.580
And I worked in a semiconductor firm before
actually transitioning into policy.

00:09:54.580 --> 00:10:04.329
So I know for a fact that almost all of the
design from major American firms like US-firms

00:10:04.329 --> 00:10:11.190
like Intel, Qualcomm, NVIDIA, AMD, everything
like even from Taiwanese firms, even from

00:10:11.190 --> 00:10:16.310
South Korean firms such as Samsung, so all
these designs are done by Indian engineers

00:10:16.310 --> 00:10:18.360
who are sitting in the country.

00:10:18.360 --> 00:10:24.279
So most of these semiconductor firms have
their design centers in India, and that is

00:10:24.279 --> 00:10:26.740
being done by Indian engineers.

00:10:26.740 --> 00:10:30.390
So would you say - looking ahead to the future,
that there's actually more potential in the

00:10:30.390 --> 00:10:34.290
design side of things than the manufacturing
when it comes to India's role?

00:10:34.290 --> 00:10:41.160
Because if you think of just you were mentioning
about how China is trying to push to be self-sufficient

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:44.589
across the supply chain.

00:10:44.589 --> 00:10:51.920
Surely it would take such a long time to build
up that kind of expertise that China has in

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:53.450
producing models.

00:10:53.450 --> 00:10:59.430
You know, could India even go down that route
in the long term?

00:10:59.430 --> 00:11:04.360
Again: So semiconductor self-sufficiency is
such an ambitious task.

00:11:04.360 --> 00:11:09.170
Like no one has been able to achieve that.

00:11:09.170 --> 00:11:16.490
Not even the biggest tech powers in the country,
not US, not Europe, not China, because each

00:11:16.490 --> 00:11:24.029
aspect of the supply chain is so, so, so intricate
and fragile, because there are so many different

00:11:24.029 --> 00:11:25.459
things which come into the picture.

00:11:25.459 --> 00:11:32.060
And as you said, it has taken Taiwan almost
50 years to actually build that semiconductor

00:11:32.060 --> 00:11:35.190
industry, which is the manufacturing bit.

00:11:35.190 --> 00:11:41.839
And the TSMC and UMC, which are their biggest
firms, which are the contract manufacturers

00:11:41.839 --> 00:11:47.890
for Taiwan, have been created and set up almost
since the 1960s.

00:11:47.890 --> 00:11:54.060
And to reach where they have reached now,
it has taken significant amount of capital

00:11:54.060 --> 00:12:00.550
investment, trade policy changes and it's
a huge - it's like a marathon.

00:12:00.550 --> 00:12:02.399
It's not a sprint.

00:12:02.399 --> 00:12:09.139
And India, again, having a fab in India is
always a good thing, because you can actually

00:12:09.139 --> 00:12:12.750
manufacture for the domestic consumption itself.

00:12:12.750 --> 00:12:15.390
That is definitely there.

00:12:15.390 --> 00:12:21.720
But again, like what kind of fab it is because
the high end fab takes so much amount of investments

00:12:21.720 --> 00:12:23.399
to actually set up.

00:12:23.399 --> 00:12:31.410
So that is why design itself is a low hanging
fruit for India, because they already have

00:12:31.410 --> 00:12:32.600
the talent in the country.

00:12:32.600 --> 00:12:35.459
They just do not have the semiconductor firms.

00:12:35.459 --> 00:12:42.779
Most of them work for American or basically
not Indian firms.

00:12:42.779 --> 00:12:49.640
And so if India can actually nurture that
talent and kind of build domestic or domestic

00:12:49.640 --> 00:12:54.389
sector, thriving sector of semiconductor design
firms, that means that India would kind of

00:12:54.389 --> 00:12:58.180
basically be the contract designers.

00:12:58.180 --> 00:13:04.660
Similarly, how Taiwan is a contract manufacturer,
all the firms can actually get their designs

00:13:04.660 --> 00:13:09.990
done by Indian firms and then they can move
on to manufacturing the chip itself.

00:13:09.990 --> 00:13:13.250
I have to say, I love the way you keep using
the word "fab".

00:13:13.250 --> 00:13:15.470
I mean, I've used "fab" for something else.

00:13:15.470 --> 00:13:16.820
For me, "fab" is fabulous, right?

00:13:16.820 --> 00:13:18.620
But this is fabrication centre.

00:13:18.620 --> 00:13:25.180
To put it into context and the way you've
been explaining about the chips, the semiconductors,

00:13:25.180 --> 00:13:31.030
I think if there were a mascot for globalization,
it should be semiconductor chips, because

00:13:31.030 --> 00:13:33.510
you can't have one country doing everything.

00:13:33.510 --> 00:13:36.610
And you've talked about India having expertise
in design.

00:13:36.610 --> 00:13:41.829
But if it is just design and if for one country
to have a production line, it takes such a

00:13:41.829 --> 00:13:44.380
long time and so much of expertise.

00:13:44.380 --> 00:13:50.880
Why was India chosen, if I may use the word
chosen, for the billion-dollar investment?

00:13:50.880 --> 00:13:56.830
So I think it was in December 2021 that Indian,
the Indian government, the Ministry of Electronics

00:13:56.830 --> 00:14:00.769
and IT came out with the semiconductor fab
ecosystem.

00:14:00.769 --> 00:14:07.759
One of their major, major goals is to get
a fab in the country, because we do not have

00:14:07.759 --> 00:14:12.750
any commercial fab right now and it is hurting
us majorly, because we were so totally dependent

00:14:12.750 --> 00:14:16.290
on imports and our biggest imports are from
China itself.

00:14:16.290 --> 00:14:19.660
And that is something that the government
wants to change.

00:14:19.660 --> 00:14:24.320
In the semiconductor industry, even a billion
dollars is just peanuts.

00:14:24.320 --> 00:14:30.170
It´s: You cannot set up a high-end fabrication
facility with a billion dollars.

00:14:30.170 --> 00:14:37.600
Like you need immense amount of capital to
just set up a basic functioning unit of a

00:14:37.600 --> 00:14:45.240
fab for, let's say, a 28-nanometer chip, which
is a mid-range.

00:14:45.240 --> 00:14:51.981
So as you decrease the nanometers, the more
transistors are put onto the chip and the

00:14:51.981 --> 00:14:53.700
more computing power is there.

00:14:53.700 --> 00:14:59.800
So currently we are at five nanometers, three
nanometers, which is too expensive.

00:14:59.800 --> 00:15:05.130
India cannot afford to put so much capital
into just one fabrication facility.

00:15:05.130 --> 00:15:10.740
And very, very few players are actually involved
in kind of doing this.

00:15:10.740 --> 00:15:16.170
There are not many players TSMC, Samsung,
Intel, these are the only major players in

00:15:16.170 --> 00:15:22.970
the whole world who can actually do a five
nanometer or three nanometer fabrication facility

00:15:22.970 --> 00:15:23.970
itself.

00:15:23.970 --> 00:15:29.380
So India does not have that amount of capital
and they have not been able to actually attract

00:15:29.380 --> 00:15:35.370
these big firms to set up any kind of fabrication
facility in the country.

00:15:35.370 --> 00:15:38.810
From what you're saying about how much it
would actually cost to create the especially

00:15:38.810 --> 00:15:43.079
these high-end chips, do you think it's very
or rather less likely then that we're really

00:15:43.079 --> 00:15:48.760
going to see any of the really state of the
art high tech being produced in India in the

00:15:48.760 --> 00:15:49.760
coming years?

00:15:49.760 --> 00:15:54.100
And is that more likely to stay in Taiwan,
for example, for the time being?

00:15:54.100 --> 00:15:55.120
Yes, definitely.

00:15:55.120 --> 00:16:02.500
It's not going to come to India anytime soon,
and it's because it's concentrated with, like

00:16:02.500 --> 00:16:07.790
very, very like just two or three firms across
the world.

00:16:07.790 --> 00:16:15.699
And this technology also is not easily transferable,
because you can't just say that, oh, I'll

00:16:15.699 --> 00:16:19.829
tell you how to do it, and you can do it because
there's so many other factors that come into

00:16:19.829 --> 00:16:20.829
the picture.

00:16:20.829 --> 00:16:25.490
So there is so many dependencies which have
been created.

00:16:25.490 --> 00:16:30.170
And India cannot like, you know, plug holes
into all of them right now.

00:16:30.170 --> 00:16:36.650
They kind of have to focus their resources
on their strengths, which is their comparative

00:16:36.650 --> 00:16:38.870
advantages also in the in the value chain.

00:16:38.870 --> 00:16:43.360
I'm actually quite intrigued to understand
how this is going to affect the job market,

00:16:43.360 --> 00:16:46.300
because semiconductors is a huge business.

00:16:46.300 --> 00:16:52.410
And even whatever level you're talking about,
if it's entering the Indian market, that is

00:16:52.410 --> 00:16:57.200
probably going to take a big chunk of the
job market, isn't it?

00:16:57.200 --> 00:17:02.449
I mean, are we going to see less rate of unemployment
because of this?

00:17:02.449 --> 00:17:08.689
Do you have any idea how the government plans
are as far as employment is concerned?

00:17:08.689 --> 00:17:13.990
So, I mean, that's the thing, right?

00:17:13.990 --> 00:17:19.990
So in each part of the value chain, you kind
of you need different required skill sets

00:17:19.990 --> 00:17:20.990
also.

00:17:20.990 --> 00:17:24.760
So for especially manufacturing, you need
experienced labor.

00:17:24.760 --> 00:17:34.470
You need kind of people who have the like
they're well, well-educated enough to understand

00:17:34.470 --> 00:17:41.730
how to work manufacturing equipment, like
a fabrication equipment itself.

00:17:41.730 --> 00:17:51.840
But for an assembly or testing or a packaging
part, you do not need that level of education

00:17:51.840 --> 00:17:53.400
in your laborers itself.

00:17:53.400 --> 00:17:58.299
It can be, they can be like, this is just
low-cost labor.

00:17:58.299 --> 00:18:03.220
And that is why India can kind of get into
that rather than this.

00:18:03.220 --> 00:18:05.409
And especially in design, it's very, very
technical.

00:18:05.409 --> 00:18:06.409
Right.

00:18:06.409 --> 00:18:12.971
So these are basically engineers who have
come out or just come out of college who are

00:18:12.971 --> 00:18:22.440
looking to contribute to the semiconductor
industry and all the opportunities they have

00:18:22.440 --> 00:18:29.240
now are either directly with the US or like
basically non-Indian firms or with services

00:18:29.240 --> 00:18:30.240
firms.

00:18:30.240 --> 00:18:35.320
There are a lot of semiconductor design services
firms in the country.

00:18:35.320 --> 00:18:41.809
Then they take a project of the bigger players,
right?

00:18:41.809 --> 00:18:44.480
So like Texas Instruments or Intel or something.

00:18:44.480 --> 00:18:49.590
They say, we will take up your project and
we'll design it for you and they'll hire engineers

00:18:49.590 --> 00:18:54.800
from colleges and the universities to do that.

00:18:54.800 --> 00:18:55.800
So.

00:18:55.800 --> 00:18:57.380
Uh, that's the thing.

00:18:57.380 --> 00:19:04.600
India needs a thriving domestic industry of
design firms itself, which we do not have

00:19:04.600 --> 00:19:05.600
right now.

00:19:05.600 --> 00:19:12.039
So once we can get that up and running, then
it will be: Yeah, it will create a lot of

00:19:12.039 --> 00:19:16.280
employment opportunities for engineers itself.

00:19:16.280 --> 00:19:22.460
And if they end up actually building a fab,
that will actually give a lot of employment

00:19:22.460 --> 00:19:23.740
to manufacturing laborers.

00:19:23.740 --> 00:19:24.740
Right.

00:19:24.740 --> 00:19:30.210
So probably we're going to see more investment
in training in the coming years and it might

00:19:30.210 --> 00:19:31.770
not happen all of a sudden.

00:19:31.770 --> 00:19:36.309
It'll take a lot of time, but there would
be more jobs, the way I understand it.

00:19:36.309 --> 00:19:37.309
Thanks a lot.

00:19:37.309 --> 00:19:38.380
It was very insightful.

00:19:38.380 --> 00:19:39.910
It was "fab".

00:19:39.910 --> 00:19:43.810
Thanks a lot for your time.

00:19:43.810 --> 00:19:44.870
Thank you.

00:19:44.870 --> 00:19:50.420
I think definitely the biggest takeaway for
me from that was how this is very much a marathon

00:19:50.420 --> 00:19:51.450
and not a sprint.

00:19:51.450 --> 00:19:53.950
And it's going to take so long to build up
that kind of expertise.

00:19:53.950 --> 00:19:58.080
When you think of the decades that it's taken
Taiwan and I think it's really going to depend

00:19:58.080 --> 00:20:02.210
now on where India decides to invest.

00:20:02.210 --> 00:20:03.919
The opportunities are there.

00:20:03.919 --> 00:20:09.210
We heard from Arjun whether it's opportunities
for engineers or even for manufacturing labor.

00:20:09.210 --> 00:20:12.070
But the focus right now, it seems, or the
biggest potential, immediate, short-term potential

00:20:12.070 --> 00:20:13.200
is design.

00:20:13.200 --> 00:20:19.340
And it depends on how and to what extent India
really jumps on that now.

00:20:19.340 --> 00:20:25.000
So it really depends where and to what extent
India decides to invest in these different

00:20:25.000 --> 00:20:26.000
areas.

00:20:26.000 --> 00:20:27.000
That's right.

00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:31.400
And I think what I've really understood is
that multilateralism is the need of this industry.

00:20:31.400 --> 00:20:34.720
So you would have one player who's good at
designing, like you just said; there's another

00:20:34.720 --> 00:20:40.309
one who's actually got the raw material, yet
another one who can do the testing and manufacturing.

00:20:40.309 --> 00:20:45.010
There would be someone else with the manpower,
but there can't be one player who can do everything.

00:20:45.010 --> 00:20:50.130
So it has to be multilateral, and one can't
really strive to be self-sufficient.

00:20:50.130 --> 00:20:51.420
That's my one takeaway.

00:20:51.420 --> 00:20:55.460
And the other thing is when it comes to jobs,
which was our question in the beginning, that

00:20:55.460 --> 00:21:00.840
jobs will be created, but probably in another
decade or so and not instantly.

00:21:00.840 --> 00:21:05.500
Now we've understood the technicalities and
he being an electronic engineer, I think he's

00:21:05.500 --> 00:21:08.600
really made us understand the technicalities
really well.

00:21:08.600 --> 00:21:13.550
But let's try and figure out the politics
that's happening behind this whole technical

00:21:13.550 --> 00:21:14.550
business.

00:21:14.550 --> 00:21:16.790
For that, let's bring in our next guest.

00:21:16.790 --> 00:21:22.510
So to understand the intricacies between India
and Taiwan relations, I'd like to bring in

00:21:22.510 --> 00:21:28.010
Mr. Harsh V. Pant and he is the vice president
of Observer Research Foundation, ORF in New

00:21:28.010 --> 00:21:29.010
Delhi.

00:21:29.010 --> 00:21:30.010
Welcome to Global Eyes.

00:21:30.010 --> 00:21:34.289
Now, Kate and I, we've been talking about
iPhone production, we've been talking about

00:21:34.289 --> 00:21:35.289
semiconductors.

00:21:35.289 --> 00:21:41.020
And we know that since this September, iPhones
and in fact, new iPhones are being made in

00:21:41.020 --> 00:21:42.020
India.

00:21:42.020 --> 00:21:44.960
I'd like to begin by asking you, how do you
really see this?

00:21:44.960 --> 00:21:51.059
Is it safe to say that this is already part
of the shift away from dependency on China

00:21:51.059 --> 00:21:54.370
centered supply chains?

00:21:54.370 --> 00:22:02.650
I think it's the beginning of China plus one
track in some ways in supply chain diversification.

00:22:02.650 --> 00:22:10.710
And we know that we live in an age where geo-economics
is being shaped by geopolitics and the sense

00:22:10.710 --> 00:22:14.529
that you cannot be over dependent on any one
particular country.

00:22:14.529 --> 00:22:20.581
And in particular China, I think, has changed
the dynamic of the supply chains: The way

00:22:20.581 --> 00:22:26.350
we think about supply chains, the way we think
about restructuring them and balancing them.

00:22:26.350 --> 00:22:30.450
And increasingly, there has been this idea
that India is perhaps one of the few countries,

00:22:30.450 --> 00:22:36.610
that provides the economies of scale in ways
that few countries, few other countries do,

00:22:36.610 --> 00:22:39.960
when you look at when you look at the shift
away from China.

00:22:39.960 --> 00:22:45.520
So I think a lot of the companies are interested
in that, in looking at India through that,

00:22:45.520 --> 00:22:46.890
through a newer lens.

00:22:46.890 --> 00:22:53.320
And I think India also wants to be part of
a new story in global geo-economics, where

00:22:53.320 --> 00:22:56.370
it presents itself as a credible production
house.

00:22:56.370 --> 00:23:01.870
So a number of themes that India is interested
in, like strengthening its domestic manufacturing

00:23:01.870 --> 00:23:06.920
base, providing alternative production linked
incentives to its own companies, as well as

00:23:06.920 --> 00:23:10.120
welcoming foreign companies to come and invest
in India.

00:23:10.120 --> 00:23:12.940
I think that has begun to take shape.

00:23:12.940 --> 00:23:18.789
To what extent it goes forward, how extensive
and detailed that process is, that remains

00:23:18.789 --> 00:23:19.789
to be seen.

00:23:19.789 --> 00:23:24.270
But certainly I think we are beginning to
look at things in a different way.

00:23:24.270 --> 00:23:29.400
And India remains at the heart of this new
supply chain restructuring.

00:23:29.400 --> 00:23:34.110
So before we get into that a little bit more,
I just want to jump to one of your papers.

00:23:34.110 --> 00:23:39.590
In the summer, you published an article in
Foreign Policy, where you wrote: "public enthusiasm

00:23:39.590 --> 00:23:43.030
and semiconductors do not make a bilateral
relationship".

00:23:43.030 --> 00:23:46.621
I'm just wondering if you could expand on
that a little bit more for us and explain

00:23:46.621 --> 00:23:49.909
exactly what you meant by that.

00:23:49.909 --> 00:23:57.169
I think what we meant by that is, look, there
is a there's a great degree of interest today

00:23:57.169 --> 00:24:00.320
in Taiwan as far as India is concerned.

00:24:00.320 --> 00:24:07.170
Indians have begun to look at Taiwan as a
nation that is very capable, that has done

00:24:07.170 --> 00:24:14.520
a remarkable job when it comes to covert management
and is increasingly at the heart of contemporary

00:24:14.520 --> 00:24:17.700
geo-technology and emerging technologies.

00:24:17.700 --> 00:24:24.150
And I think that interest in itself is producing
a greater sense of anticipation about these

00:24:24.150 --> 00:24:25.150
ties.

00:24:25.150 --> 00:24:30.930
And on the other hand, you have this whole
debate about semiconductors and supply chain

00:24:30.930 --> 00:24:36.000
restructuring, about semiconductors, and whether
semiconductor partnership between India and

00:24:36.000 --> 00:24:40.120
Taiwan can be the nucleus of a broader partnership.

00:24:40.120 --> 00:24:45.190
But what in fact, we were arguing in that
piece was, that India needs to look at Taiwan

00:24:45.190 --> 00:24:47.159
more holistically.

00:24:47.159 --> 00:24:52.320
Because Taiwan and what happens in and around
Taiwan in the coming years and decades will

00:24:52.320 --> 00:24:58.059
also determine the future balance of power
in what we now call as the Indo-Pacific.

00:24:58.059 --> 00:25:05.450
And unless India is more vocal, unless India
is more politically active in engaging with

00:25:05.450 --> 00:25:10.050
Taiwan, I think there are going to be, you
know, this public enthusiasm and this newfound

00:25:10.050 --> 00:25:15.270
interest in semiconductors will pay some dividends,
but not to the extent that perhaps many people

00:25:15.270 --> 00:25:16.420
in India anticipate.

00:25:16.420 --> 00:25:21.600
Now, I'd like to ask if India keeps getting
closer to Taiwan, the trade relations are

00:25:21.600 --> 00:25:25.679
getting better and if I were to quote from
your paper, you've written that bilateral

00:25:25.679 --> 00:25:30.640
trade with Taiwan stood at $5.7 billion in
2020, and it continues to grow.

00:25:30.640 --> 00:25:35.200
And we're not only talking about private Taiwanese
companies who are trying to invest in India,

00:25:35.200 --> 00:25:39.511
but it is also at the governments level that
both the countries, Taiwan and India, they're

00:25:39.511 --> 00:25:40.511
trying to come closer.

00:25:40.511 --> 00:25:45.510
I want to understand, can that be seen as
a deliberate attempt to irritate China?

00:25:45.510 --> 00:25:47.850
Well, you know, there are two things.

00:25:47.850 --> 00:25:57.100
One is, of course, India's relationship with
Taiwan, where trade and cultural ties have

00:25:57.100 --> 00:25:59.410
long been part of India's engagement with
Taiwan.

00:25:59.410 --> 00:26:03.980
India has a cultural centre, Indian trade
and cultural centre in Taiwan.

00:26:03.980 --> 00:26:07.190
Taiwan has a similar centre in India.

00:26:07.190 --> 00:26:14.370
Both countries interact at the level of educational
exchanges, people to people ties, trade and

00:26:14.370 --> 00:26:17.050
commerce, as well as broader cultural interaction.

00:26:17.050 --> 00:26:22.720
So there has been a sense that India needs
to cultivate and continue to engage Taiwan

00:26:22.720 --> 00:26:29.850
as an entity, even in the context of one China
policy that India has articulated.

00:26:29.850 --> 00:26:35.790
But at the other side of this is that in the
recent years, certainly many in India have

00:26:35.790 --> 00:26:42.570
questioned India's continued adherence to
One China policy, when many in India believe

00:26:42.570 --> 00:26:47.029
that China, in fact, never adheres to one
India policy.

00:26:47.029 --> 00:26:55.580
And that has been a criticism that even Indian
policy makers have made openly that the relationship

00:26:55.580 --> 00:27:02.020
between India and China has to be more reciprocal.

00:27:02.020 --> 00:27:06.940
It cannot be a one-way street where India
continues to give in, whereas China continues

00:27:06.940 --> 00:27:11.320
to demand more from India by unilaterally
changing the status quo along the border,

00:27:11.320 --> 00:27:15.399
by challenging India across its periphery,
etc.

00:27:15.399 --> 00:27:21.809
So I think that articulation that's a sense
that India needs to push back in certain ways

00:27:21.809 --> 00:27:25.990
has also now given a new dynamic to India
-Taiwan relations.

00:27:25.990 --> 00:27:32.070
Certainly India would like to increase the
engagement with Taiwan and Taiwan in particular

00:27:32.070 --> 00:27:37.779
also is interested in increasing its engagement
with a country like India, because as I mentioned,

00:27:37.779 --> 00:27:42.640
you know, apart from China, there are few
other countries apart from India that can

00:27:42.640 --> 00:27:48.090
give Chinese industries, whether they are
semiconductor industry or the electronics

00:27:48.090 --> 00:27:54.019
industry, the kind of economies of scale,
and that perhaps a big market like India or

00:27:54.019 --> 00:27:55.630
a big country like India can provide.

00:27:55.630 --> 00:28:00.580
I wonder, though, traditionally India has
always tried to keep equidistant in a way

00:28:00.580 --> 00:28:05.909
between different powers, different blocs,
while still being able to be part of some

00:28:05.909 --> 00:28:09.409
cooperative organization, so to speak.

00:28:09.409 --> 00:28:15.350
So I'm wondering, how do you think these new
technological and economic ties between India

00:28:15.350 --> 00:28:23.770
and Taiwan might affect this balancing act
of of Delhi's?

00:28:23.770 --> 00:28:32.240
I think, you know, when it comes to China
today, there is there is no attempt to hide

00:28:32.240 --> 00:28:33.730
India's frustration.

00:28:33.730 --> 00:28:39.830
In New Delhi, policy makers are frustrated,
are anxious.

00:28:39.830 --> 00:28:45.870
And I think ordinary people in India are also
very worried about Chinese behavior.

00:28:45.870 --> 00:28:53.940
What happened in the Galwan Valley in in 2020,
where there was a major scuffle between the

00:28:53.940 --> 00:29:01.240
two sides and soldiers on both sides died
first time in decades when there was violence

00:29:01.240 --> 00:29:05.360
on the border has really shaken the Indian
perception of China.

00:29:05.360 --> 00:29:10.340
In the past, also there were differences and
there was an acknowledgement of differences.

00:29:10.340 --> 00:29:15.940
But the fact that we now have a very volatile
border with China where there is violence,

00:29:15.940 --> 00:29:21.169
where there are deaths, where there is a sense
of apprehension of of what might come, that

00:29:21.169 --> 00:29:24.590
has, I think, changed the nature of this relationship
fundamentally.

00:29:24.590 --> 00:29:29.909
So I don't think there is any question that
India would not take those actions that are

00:29:29.909 --> 00:29:34.899
needed to protect and preserve what India
believes are its core interests vis a vis

00:29:34.899 --> 00:29:35.899
China.

00:29:35.899 --> 00:29:39.150
And China has been also pushing India on a
number of fronts.

00:29:39.150 --> 00:29:44.010
So what we have seen in the last few years
is India getting closer to like-minded countries

00:29:44.010 --> 00:29:48.940
in the Indo-Pacific, whether it is the quadrilateral
security dialogue with the US, Australia,

00:29:48.940 --> 00:29:53.929
Japan, whether it is conducting military exercises
with like-minded countries or whether it is

00:29:53.929 --> 00:29:59.039
giving a signaling to China that it is willing
to engage Taiwan much more substantively than

00:29:59.039 --> 00:30:05.580
before, if only to make a case to China that
India also has some cards on the table.

00:30:05.580 --> 00:30:11.659
It is not as devoid of options in case China
becomes more aggressive or more assertive.

00:30:11.659 --> 00:30:17.380
And in case China becomes more aggressive,
now, chances are that there could be armed

00:30:17.380 --> 00:30:19.640
conflict in the Taiwan Strait.

00:30:19.640 --> 00:30:24.510
So hypothetically talking, if that were to
happen, if China were to use military force

00:30:24.510 --> 00:30:27.870
against Taiwan, how would India position itself?

00:30:27.870 --> 00:30:31.390
Kate has just said she called it a balancing
act.

00:30:31.390 --> 00:30:36.840
How would India balance itself in in the world
order?

00:30:36.840 --> 00:30:44.039
I think a lot depends on China's behavior
over the coming years.

00:30:44.039 --> 00:30:52.420
If China continues to be this assertive, this
aggressive, the kind of behavior that China

00:30:52.420 --> 00:30:57.149
is pursuing vis a vis India today, if that
continues where China does not even acknowledge

00:30:57.149 --> 00:31:03.350
Indian sensitivities, then I think India will
be more strident, both in the criticism of

00:31:03.350 --> 00:31:06.780
Chinese behavior as well as in its support
for Taiwan.

00:31:06.780 --> 00:31:12.590
The question is, you know, what other countries
are going to do, because India in and of itself

00:31:12.590 --> 00:31:17.639
cannot change the dynamic vis a vis Taiwan
unless other countries, other democracies,

00:31:17.639 --> 00:31:24.059
other like-minded entities in the region,
unless European Union, unless the US start

00:31:24.059 --> 00:31:29.980
becoming more vocal about Taiwan, I don't
think India can change the fundamental reality

00:31:29.980 --> 00:31:38.020
of Taiwan being, you know, a very small country
near a behemoth like China, and that will

00:31:38.020 --> 00:31:39.760
have implications for Taiwanese future.

00:31:39.760 --> 00:31:46.230
So I think a lot will depend on how the international
community, how the democratic world in particular

00:31:46.230 --> 00:31:48.799
reacts to what happens in Taiwan Straits.

00:31:48.799 --> 00:31:52.900
I mean, looking at the market right now, looking
at the supply chains right now, do you think

00:31:52.900 --> 00:31:59.399
that EU will speak against China if, hypothetically
again, that situation were to arise?

00:31:59.399 --> 00:32:10.950
Well, it's very unlikely and we have also
seen EU taking a very, very balancing attitude.

00:32:10.950 --> 00:32:16.320
I think Kate used that word for India, but
I think it equally applies for Europe.

00:32:16.320 --> 00:32:23.590
We have had German chancellor visiting China
despite criticism from some of Germany's closest

00:32:23.590 --> 00:32:26.190
allies from within the government, etc.

00:32:26.190 --> 00:32:31.019
So I think we know that there are divisions
within within European Union, within Europe

00:32:31.019 --> 00:32:34.740
about the future of the relationship with
China.

00:32:34.740 --> 00:32:39.850
And therefore, what I was saying was that
it would depend on what the Western world,

00:32:39.850 --> 00:32:46.230
what the democratic world's reaction to what
happens in Taiwan is when there is a crisis.

00:32:46.230 --> 00:32:50.980
The more united the democratic world is, the
better the chances are that one can provide

00:32:50.980 --> 00:32:54.250
some support, some credible support to Taiwan.

00:32:54.250 --> 00:32:59.870
But I think certainly given the dependencies,
given the supply chains at the moment and

00:32:59.870 --> 00:33:05.010
given that strategic or economic decoupling
is really a long-term process, it would take

00:33:05.010 --> 00:33:06.010
time.

00:33:06.010 --> 00:33:07.520
And even India is aware of that.

00:33:07.520 --> 00:33:11.720
India, for example, since 2020 has been trying
to make a case or has been trying to reduce

00:33:11.720 --> 00:33:13.480
its dependence on China.

00:33:13.480 --> 00:33:16.539
But it's very difficult.

00:33:16.539 --> 00:33:24.139
India's trade with China is very high even
today, and it's trying to change that matrix,

00:33:24.139 --> 00:33:25.640
but it's going to take a long time.

00:33:25.640 --> 00:33:27.279
And I think the same would apply to Europe.

00:33:27.279 --> 00:33:31.299
I just want to jump on a different point that
you mentioned there very briefly, which is

00:33:31.299 --> 00:33:37.190
this economic decoupling, which makes me want
to get back actually to the geopolitics of

00:33:37.190 --> 00:33:38.299
these semiconductors.

00:33:38.299 --> 00:33:44.980
I mean, they're really seen as being so key
to the future in the coming decades, whether

00:33:44.980 --> 00:33:50.970
it's through mastering emerging technologies
like AI and really, you know, they will play

00:33:50.970 --> 00:33:56.289
such a key role in determining who really
has the upper hand in the 21st century.

00:33:56.289 --> 00:34:02.150
You know, if you look back to the United States
just a couple of years ago, already in 2020,

00:34:02.150 --> 00:34:09.899
the US imposed this embargo on the export
of American semiconductor technology to all

00:34:09.899 --> 00:34:13.020
Chinese semiconductor firms.

00:34:13.020 --> 00:34:20.570
We saw more restrictions and export controls
also related to semiconductors imposed just

00:34:20.570 --> 00:34:23.500
a couple of months ago in October of this
year.

00:34:23.500 --> 00:34:28.929
So I'm wondering effectively, this is, you
know, in this example, it's the US trying

00:34:28.929 --> 00:34:33.669
to stop the flow of American technology to
China.

00:34:33.669 --> 00:34:40.330
I'm wondering what is India's place in Washington's
plan right now?

00:34:40.330 --> 00:34:51.330
Well, I think India is increasingly and of
course, as you mentioned, the emerging technologies

00:34:51.330 --> 00:34:55.099
are at the heart of this geopolitical contestation
between US and China.

00:34:55.099 --> 00:35:01.369
And US, as you mentioned, very rightly, has
been making a case not only within its in

00:35:01.369 --> 00:35:06.109
its domestic legislation, but also to its
partners and allies that we need to relook

00:35:06.109 --> 00:35:10.680
at our relationship with China when it comes
to emerging and strategic technologies.

00:35:10.680 --> 00:35:15.190
And therefore, a lot of constraints are being
put into place, a lot of a framework is being

00:35:15.190 --> 00:35:24.181
put in place that would make it virtually
impossible for China to leverage these kinds

00:35:24.181 --> 00:35:27.450
of technological advantages from the US system.

00:35:27.450 --> 00:35:35.099
Now, I think India, on the other hand, is
seen as a vital partner of the US and we have

00:35:35.099 --> 00:35:42.230
seen India becoming a beneficiary of this
move away from China factor that that we are

00:35:42.230 --> 00:35:43.230
witnessing.

00:35:43.230 --> 00:35:44.230
Right.

00:35:44.230 --> 00:35:47.500
So you've said a couple of times that you
cannot be dependent on any one country.

00:35:47.500 --> 00:35:49.730
There's a need to reduce dependency.

00:35:49.730 --> 00:35:55.540
And you just mentioned that India, US and
India, EU relationships will have to be worked

00:35:55.540 --> 00:35:56.540
on.

00:35:56.540 --> 00:36:01.480
Now I would like to wrap up with this that
recently Xi Jinping has said that in one of

00:36:01.480 --> 00:36:08.690
his speeches that the country needs to become
self-reliant and China needs to strengthen

00:36:08.690 --> 00:36:10.740
in the field of science and technology.

00:36:10.740 --> 00:36:14.339
Now, that is also something that the Indian
Prime Minister likes to say a lot, that there

00:36:14.339 --> 00:36:18.140
should be this Atmanirbhar Bharat self-reliant
India.

00:36:18.140 --> 00:36:24.170
So when it comes to semiconductors, my question
is, is it even practically possible to be

00:36:24.170 --> 00:36:31.070
self-reliant, to be completely independent
of others in this business?

00:36:31.070 --> 00:36:32.290
I don't think so.

00:36:32.290 --> 00:36:40.660
And I think that is why, you know, India,
for example, is so keen on Taiwan and a relationship

00:36:40.660 --> 00:36:46.260
with Taiwan at this juncture, in particular
when it comes to semiconductor industry, because

00:36:46.260 --> 00:36:53.109
certainly with all the emphasis on self-reliance
and Atmanirbhar Bharat, as you mentioned,

00:36:53.109 --> 00:37:01.070
the idea actually has been that India's manufacturing
has been getting decimated over the last few

00:37:01.070 --> 00:37:02.070
decades.

00:37:02.070 --> 00:37:08.530
And this is the story of other major economic
powers as well, where we have seen China's

00:37:08.530 --> 00:37:13.260
manufacturing capability growing, whereas
the manufacturing capacities of other countries

00:37:13.260 --> 00:37:16.440
virtually almost disappearing.

00:37:16.440 --> 00:37:21.859
And that has, I think, reignited this debate
as to how do we balance our economies better

00:37:21.859 --> 00:37:28.160
so that the sustainability of our economic
engagement, the sort of lopsided economic

00:37:28.160 --> 00:37:33.530
engagement that many countries have had with
China can be reduced.

00:37:33.530 --> 00:37:38.960
And that is where this thing about self-reliance
comes from, because India, like many other

00:37:38.960 --> 00:37:42.990
countries, wants to rebuild its manufacturing
base.

00:37:42.990 --> 00:37:48.310
Now, in certain sectors, that's going to be
easy, in certain sectors, India has moved

00:37:48.310 --> 00:37:49.310
ahead.

00:37:49.310 --> 00:37:52.510
But in certain sectors, as you rightly mentioned,
like semiconductors, it's not possible to

00:37:52.510 --> 00:37:56.030
do that, particularly in the short to medium
term.

00:37:56.030 --> 00:38:02.970
And you have to create an entire ecosystem
that can generate the industrial infrastructure

00:38:02.970 --> 00:38:03.970
for that.

00:38:03.970 --> 00:38:05.560
And that's not going to happen overnight.

00:38:05.560 --> 00:38:08.320
And therefore partnerships are going to be
important.

00:38:08.320 --> 00:38:12.839
But partnerships again, would mean that how
do you define those partnerships?

00:38:12.839 --> 00:38:14.950
Where do you go for those partnerships?

00:38:14.950 --> 00:38:20.490
And I think most of the world is recognizing
the fact that instead of putting all your

00:38:20.490 --> 00:38:23.950
eggs in one basket, it's better to diversify.

00:38:23.950 --> 00:38:29.080
And in particular, you go to those countries
where you feel there is a greater degree of

00:38:29.080 --> 00:38:30.080
trust and transparency.

00:38:30.080 --> 00:38:32.870
I think, unfortunately, we're going to have
to wrap up there.

00:38:32.870 --> 00:38:37.680
But thank you so much for your time and for
your insights.

00:38:37.680 --> 00:38:38.680
Thank you very much.

00:38:38.680 --> 00:38:39.680
Thank you.

00:38:39.680 --> 00:38:45.490
I think if one thing is clear, certainly partnerships
and diversifying are really the two key words

00:38:45.490 --> 00:38:52.950
to take away from this right now as this economic
decoupling from China, not only by countries

00:38:52.950 --> 00:38:57.940
like Germany and the US, but this is really
on a global scale and there's loads of huge

00:38:57.940 --> 00:38:59.040
challenges there as well.

00:38:59.040 --> 00:39:04.230
But of course, at the same time we have the
situation where you have these growing tensions

00:39:04.230 --> 00:39:05.800
between China and Taiwan.

00:39:05.800 --> 00:39:12.859
And as we heard from both of our really great
guests, we can see already how fragile the

00:39:12.859 --> 00:39:15.319
supply chain is in creating these chips.

00:39:15.319 --> 00:39:21.550
And so what a disturbance in the manufacturing
of these chips in Taiwan, for example, were

00:39:21.550 --> 00:39:27.300
there to be any kind of Chinese aggression
towards Taiwan, what that would mean on a

00:39:27.300 --> 00:39:29.110
global scale.

00:39:29.110 --> 00:39:32.850
And it's really underlines, again, after what
we've already seen with the shortages at the

00:39:32.850 --> 00:39:40.700
height of the pandemic, just what an integral
part of life and how our future really depends

00:39:40.700 --> 00:39:47.300
on these chips, whether it's from everyday
electronics, from our phones to our toothbrushes,

00:39:47.300 --> 00:39:49.240
even to advanced weapons.

00:39:49.240 --> 00:39:56.619
So these chips and semiconductors really are
going to be so important in determining the

00:39:56.619 --> 00:40:02.750
future of not only what we use every day around
the home, but also, you know, global politics

00:40:02.750 --> 00:40:04.850
as well and our security.

00:40:04.850 --> 00:40:07.970
Our future really depends on these chips,
that's correct.

00:40:07.970 --> 00:40:13.630
And if I were to take the example of Russia,
since the war in Ukraine started, now America

00:40:13.630 --> 00:40:18.460
has made sure that Russia does not get any
chips that has got anything to do with America,

00:40:18.460 --> 00:40:23.210
whether it is design that was done in America
or whether the chips were produced there or

00:40:23.210 --> 00:40:24.570
just about anything.

00:40:24.570 --> 00:40:32.780
In India, you say, if I can again use a couple
of words in Hindi, in India they say "hukka

00:40:32.780 --> 00:40:33.780
paani band karna".

00:40:33.780 --> 00:40:38.890
Now that means when you have animosity with
someone, you make sure that they don't get

00:40:38.890 --> 00:40:40.470
water, they don't get food.

00:40:40.470 --> 00:40:45.359
But in this case, I understand when you have
to make sure that some economy should not

00:40:45.359 --> 00:40:49.350
strive, then you're going to stop giving semiconductors
to them.

00:40:49.350 --> 00:40:55.050
And another thing, one sentence that's going
to stay with me was what Harsh said, that

00:40:55.050 --> 00:40:57.720
new partnerships, they're not going to happen
overnight.

00:40:57.720 --> 00:41:00.410
So that is going to take time.

00:41:00.410 --> 00:41:05.280
But whatever time it takes, whatever course
it takes, this is really going to decide our

00:41:05.280 --> 00:41:08.282
future, semiconductor chips, the tiny, tiny,
tiny semiconductor chips.

00:41:08.282 --> 00:41:09.839
Tiny thing, huge impact.

00:41:09.839 --> 00:41:12.750
And it's going to be fab.

00:41:12.750 --> 00:41:15.770
That's something I'm going to remember.

